        "text": "ModernWallet Free Financial Calculators Calculators All calculators Auto Loan Mortgage Retirement Investing Portfolio Real Estate Net Worth Budget Tax Resolution Estate Planning Probate Elder Care Trump Account 529 College Savings Coast FIRE Business Loan Payoff Personal Loan Interest Per Day Taxable vs. Tax-Deferred Credit Card Payoff IUL Merchant Cash Advance Invoice Factoring Business Line of Credit PTO Cash-Out Zakat Life Insurance Needs Self-Employment Tax Operating Cash Flow Mileage Deduction Cash Conversion Cycle Freelance Rate HELOC S-Corp Tax Commercial Mortgage CD Calculator Emergency Fund Debt Consolidation Inflation Expense Ratio Social Security Break-Even Mileage Reimbursement Portfolio Rebalancing Hurricane Deductible Annuity Credit Card Interest Loan Payment HYSA vs CD Trump Account vs 529 Mortgage Recast Social Security COLA Biweekly Mortgage Roth vs Traditional 401(k) Pay Off Mortgage or Invest Florida Amendment 3 Student Loan Garnishment Tax Bracket Debt-to-Income Ratio Taxable Social Security Checklists Charitable Donation Deduction Salary Car Loan Interest Deduction Paycheck Rent vs Buy Stock Capital Gains Tax Hidden Cost of Owning a Home Backdoor Roth Pro Rata × ModernWallet / Backdoor Roth Pro Rata Backdoor Roth Pro Rata Rule Calculator By Jonathan Velez , Personal Finance Writer & Editor · Reviewed against primary sources · Updated November 18, 2026 Your taxable conversion share equals one minus your total basis divided by the sum of your December 31 IRA value and the amount you converted. The converted dollars are added into the bottom of that fraction. The proportion measures your balances across all traditional, Simplified Employee Pension (SEP), and Savings Incentive Match Plan for Employees (SIMPLE) individual retirement arrangements (IRAs). For example, suppose you make a $7,500 nondeductible contribution and convert the entire $7,500. If you still hold $92,500 in other traditional IRAs on December 31, your tax-free share is 0.075. That calculation leaves $562.50 tax-free and makes $6,937.50 taxable. That means 92.5% of your conversion is taxable. Share Interactive — edit any field How the Calculator Applies the Pro Rata Rule The tool uses four inputs to calculate your taxable conversion amount. Enter your nondeductible contribution for the current tax year from line 1 of Form 8606 . Add any basis from earlier years from line 2, which comes from line 14 of your prior return. Enter the total value of all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as of December 31 after the conversion, which corresponds to line 6. Enter the net amount converted to a Roth IRA during the year from line 8. The converted amount is not part of line 6. It is added to the denominator separately. Form 8606 figures the tax-free and taxable portions step by step. Line 3 adds line 1 and line 2 to find your total basis. The denominator adds your December 31 balance from line 6 to your converted amount from line 8. Dividing line 3 by that combined sum gives your nontaxable share. That decimal is rounded to at least three places and cannot exceed 1.000. Line 17 multiplies your converted amount by that share to find the tax-free basis. Line 18 subtracts line 17 from line 16 to find the taxable amount, and line 14 tracks your remaining basis for next year. In our internal modeling, we ran the Form 8606 proportion on nine balance cases and replayed the IRS's own Worksheet 1-1 illustration, which matched. In that example from IRS Publication 590-B , Rose Green has $2,300 of total basis, a December 31 value of $20,000, and converts $5,000. Her nontaxable share is 0.092. Multiplying $5,000 by 0.092 yields $460 tax-free. Her taxable conversion amount is $4,540. Boundary cases produce predictable outcomes under this formula. If your IRAs hold only after-tax basis, your nontaxable share is 1.000, leaving $0 taxable. If your IRA value drops after converting more than your basis, the formula caps the share at 1.000. If you convert nothing during the year, line 18 is $0 and your entire basis carries forward on line 14. If you leave $25,000 in your pre-tax accounts after converting $7,500, your share is 0.231. That leaves $1,732.50 tax-free and makes $5,767.50 taxable, which is 76.9%. Your balances on December 31 govern the entire tax year. A conversion is a withdrawal reinvested within 60 days into a Roth IRA under IRS Publication 590-A . Qualified workplace retirement plans may, but are not required to, accept rollovers from a traditional IRA. If your workplace plan accepts incoming rollovers, moving your pre-tax IRA balance into that plan before December 31 removes those dollars from line 6. Three Cases That Change the Result The proportion of your backdoor Roth conversion that is taxable depends on how much pre-tax IRA money you hold on December 31. Under the current Instructions for Form 8606 for the 2025 tax year, the IRS aggregates all of your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs into a single total. The table below illustrates three common conversion outcomes modeled from a $7,500 nondeductible contribution, zero prior basis, and a $7,500 conversion. Case (December 31 IRA value after conversion) Share Tax-Free Part Taxable Part Taxable Share Basis Left No pre-tax IRA money ($0) 1.000 $7,500.00 $0.00 0% $0.00 $92,500 in other IRAs 0.075 $562.50 $6,937.50 92.5% $6,937.50 $250,000 rollover IRA 0.029 $217.50 $7,282.50 97.1% $7,282.50 In the second case, leaving $92,500 in traditional IRAs dilutes your basis across a $100,000 combined denominator. Your tax-free share drops to 0.075, making $6,937.50 of the $7,500 conversion taxable. In the third case, holding a $250,000 rollover IRA pushes your nontaxable share down to 0.029. You pay tax on $7,282.50 of the $7,500 conversion. That leaves 97.1% of the transfer taxable, with $7,282.50 of basis remaining on line 14. A workplace plan rollover provides an operational alternative when available. If you roll your entire pre-tax IRA balance into an employer qualified plan that accepts rollovers before December 31, your line 6 balance becomes $0. Under IRS Publication 590-A, qualified plans are permitted to accept rollovers, but they are not required to do so. A reader whose plan accepts the rollover achieves a 0% taxable conversion, while a reader whose plan refuses the transfer remains at 97.1% taxable on a $250,000 balance. What the Result Does Not Include This calculator calculates your taxable conversion amount under federal rules, but it does not determine your final tax bill. Review the following exclusions when evaluating your conversion: Federal income tax rates and brackets: The calculator figures the dollar amount added to gross income, but does not estimate tax owed. To estimate your marginal tax bracket, use the tax bracket calculator . State and local income taxes: State tax treatment of traditional IRA distributions varies and is not included. Medicare Income-Related Monthly Adjustment Amount (IRMAA): Medicare IRMAA is outside the tool. Penalties: The tool does not calculate IRS penalties, such as the $50 penalty for failure to file Form 8606 or the $100 penalty for overstating nondeductible contributions. Contributions made after December 31: Contributions made between January 1 and the tax filing deadline require the full Form 8606 instructions. Other IRA transactions: Deductible traditional IRA contributions, disaster-distribution repayments, divorce transfers, and recharacterizations fall outside the scope of this calculator. For readers who also have access to workplace savings options, our guide to the mega backdoor Roth 401(k) covers after-tax payroll contributions. If you need step-by-step instructions on timing conversions and reporting them on your return, read our guide on Roth conversion rules . To compare account types, review our breakdown of a Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k) . Our Takeaway If every IRA dollar you own is already after-tax basis, your Roth conversion is tax-free. Every pre-tax dollar you hold in a traditional, SEP, or SIMPLE IRA on December 31 raises your taxable conversion amount in direct proportion. In our internal modeling, we ran the Form 8606 proportion on multiple balance tiers and replayed the IRS's own Worksheet 1-1 illustration, which matched. Roll your pre-tax IRA balance into a workplace qualified retirement plan that accepts it before December 31, or skip the backdoor conversion if the taxable share is higher than you want to pay now. To evaluate your own balances, enter your numbers in the backdoor Roth pro rata calculator above. Frequently asked questions What counts as a pre-tax IRA for the pro rata rule? Traditional IRAs, SEP IRAs, and SIMPLE IRAs all count as pre-tax IRAs under Form 8606 line 6. The instructions require you to aggregate the total December 31 value of all these accounts. Does a Roth IRA count in the pro rata calculation? No. Roth IRAs do not count in the pro rata calculation. Line 6 asks only for the value of your traditional IRAs, which the instructions define to include SEP and SIMPLE IRAs. Existing balances in Roth IRAs are excluded from the denominator. Can I fix the pro rata tax after I convert? No, you cannot undo a conversion after it occurs. Under IRS Publication 590-A, conversions made after 2017 cannot be recharacterized. You can lower the tax before December 31 by rolling your pre-tax balances into a workplace plan that accepts them, but you cannot fix the calculation after year end. Why does the calculator add my converted amount to my December 31 balance? The IRS formula requires you to measure your total IRA value before the conversion occurred. Adding line 8 back to line 6 reconstructs the full year-end value of your accounts. The formula then divides your basis by this combined total to determine the tax-free share. What happens if my IRAs hold only after-tax money? Your conversion is entirely tax-free. When your basis equals your total IRA value, the calculated nontaxable share is 1.000. That fraction leaves zero taxable dollars on line 18, and any remaining basis carries forward to the following tax year. What is Form 8606 line 6? Line 6 is the total value of all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as of December 31, plus outstanding rollovers. An outstanding rollover is generally a distribution received after November 1 that is rolled over within 60 days in the following year. Outstanding rollovers to qualified retirement plans are excluded. How do I lower my pre-tax IRA balance before December 31? You can lower your pre-tax IRA balance by rolling it into an employer qualified retirement plan before December 31. Under IRS Publication 590-A, qualified plans may accept rollovers from traditional IRAs, but they are not required to do so. Readers evaluating workplace options can also review our guide on the mega backdoor Roth 401(k) . Does this calculator include state tax? No. The calculator models federal rules only. State and local income taxes vary by jurisdiction and are outside the scope of this tool. You must calculate any applicable state income taxes separately. Is this tax advice? No. This page provides general federal tax information based on the tax year 2025 Form 8606 instructions. It is not personal tax advice. A qualified tax professional can help you apply these rules to your specific tax return. Sources We prioritize primary sources for rules, formulas, rates, limits, and definitions. See our calculator methodology and editorial policy . IRS Instructions for Form 8606 (tax year 2025) IRS About Form 8606 IRS Publication 590-A, Contributions to Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs) IRS Publication 590-B, Distributions from Individual Retirement Arrangements (IRAs) Plan the bigger picture How to do a backdoor Roth IRA step by step → Roth conversion rules: bracket, timing and no-undo → Find your 2026 tax bracket → Mega backdoor Roth 401(k) → All calculators → ModernWallet Free, accurate financial calculators — auto loans, mortg