            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What counts as a pre-tax IRA for the pro rata rule?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Traditional IRAs, SEP IRAs, and SIMPLE IRAs all count as pre-tax IRAs under Form 8606 line 6. The instructions require you to aggregate the total December 31 value of all these accounts."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does a Roth IRA count in the pro rata calculation?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. Roth IRAs do not count in the pro rata calculation. Line 6 asks only for the value of your traditional IRAs, which the instructions define to include SEP and SIMPLE IRAs. Existing balances in Roth IRAs are excluded from the denominator."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Can I fix the pro rata tax after I convert?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No, you cannot undo a conversion after it occurs. Under IRS Publication 590-A, conversions made after 2017 cannot be recharacterized. You can lower the tax before December 31 by rolling your pre-tax balances into a workplace plan that accepts them, but you cannot fix the calculation after year end."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Why does the calculator add my converted amount to my December 31 balance?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The IRS formula requires you to measure your total IRA value before the conversion occurred. Adding line 8 back to line 6 reconstructs the full year-end value of your accounts. The formula then divides your basis by this combined total to determine the tax-free share."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What happens if my IRAs hold only after-tax money?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Your conversion is entirely tax-free. When your basis equals your total IRA value, the calculated nontaxable share is 1.000. That fraction leaves zero taxable dollars on line 18, and any remaining basis carries forward to the following tax year."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is Form 8606 line 6?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Line 6 is the total value of all your traditional, SEP, and SIMPLE IRAs as of December 31, plus outstanding rollovers. An outstanding rollover is generally a distribution received after November 1 that is rolled over within 60 days in the following year. Outstanding rollovers to qualified retirement plans are excluded."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "How do I lower my pre-tax IRA balance before December 31?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "You can lower your pre-tax IRA balance by rolling it into an employer qualified retirement plan before December 31. Under IRS Publication 590-A, qualified plans may accept rollovers from traditional IRAs, but they are not required to do so. Readers evaluating workplace options can also review our guide on the mega backdoor Roth 401(k)."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does this calculator include state tax?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No. The calculator models federal rules only. State and local income taxes vary by jurisdiction and are outside the scope of this tool. You must calculate any applicable state income taxes separately."
                }
              },
