            "mainEntity": [
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Is a Roth 401(k) tax-free when you withdraw?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "A Roth 401(k) withdrawal is completely tax-free only if it meets the requirements for a qualified distribution. Under IRS rules, you must hold the account for at least five years starting from the first year you contributed, and you must be at least age 59½, disabled, or deceased. If you take a nonqualified distribution before meeting both conditions, the earnings portion is included in your gross income and may incur an additional 10 percent early distribution tax."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What is the downside of a Roth 401(k)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The primary downside of a designated Roth 401(k) is that your contributions are not excluded from your gross income, meaning you pay income taxes on them upfront in the year you contribute. Nonqualified distributions are split pro rata between contributions and earnings, making a portion of early withdrawals taxable. Once contributed, designated Roth contributions cannot be recharacterized as pretax contributions. Employer matching contributions cannot enter the designated Roth account and must be placed into a separate pretax account. Your withdrawals are also subject to employer plan distribution restrictions."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "What are the new IRA withdrawal rules for 2026?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "The IRS 2026 limits release IR-2025-111 raised the annual IRA contribution limit to $7,500, while workplace elective deferral limits increased to $24,500. The fundamental withdrawal tests and qualified distribution rules for designated Roth accounts and Roth IRAs remain those established in IRS publications checked on November 18, 2026."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Which is better for me, a Roth IRA or a 401(k)?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "A designated Roth 401(k) allows much higher annual contributions and has no modified adjusted gross income limits, meaning high earners can contribute directly regardless of their income level. A Roth IRA offers more flexible nonqualified withdrawal ordering rules because the IRS treats your regular contributions as coming out before earnings. You can explore how these features compare on our Roth IRA vs Roth 401(k) guide to match each structure to your current income and retirement savings goals."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Does a Roth 401(k) have required minimum distributions?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No, designated Roth accounts in a 401(k) plan do not have required minimum distributions while the account owner is alive. The IRS required minimum distributions FAQs confirm that RMD rules do not apply to Roth IRAs or designated Roth accounts during the owner's lifetime. However, RMD rules do apply to beneficiaries who inherit a designated Roth account."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Can I roll a Roth 401(k) into a Roth IRA?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "Yes, you can roll an eligible rollover distribution from a designated Roth 401(k) directly into a Roth IRA. A direct rollover moves your balance between custodians and is reported on Form 1099-R using letter code H, meaning it is not a taxable distribution to you. Rolling over preserves the tax status of your balance and subjects future distributions to the Roth IRA withdrawal ordering rules."
                }
              },
              {
                "@type": "Question",
                "name": "Is this tax advice?",
                "acceptedAnswer": {
                  "@type": "Answer",
                  "text": "No, this guide provides general federal tax information based on IRS publications and guidelines checked on November 18, 2026, and does not constitute formal tax or legal advice. Individual employer plan documents control what distributions your plan allows. A tax professional can apply this information to your specific return."
                }
              }
